expect  that we you know that we're able to  detect that so it's pretty scary i mean  i think it's one reason we really need a  strong crop insurance program  i know it's you know it's not always  enough but it's at least it keeps you  from having to dig out of quite such a  big hole  because you can't afford  you know next year we want to invest in  the next thing we don't want to dig out  of last year's hole so  you know you're dealing with an  insurance program where you have to take  the first 50 of the loss so that's i  mean  you'd be crazy if you bought an  insurance program like that for your car  or your house because it just just  wouldn't work  um  you know i think  you know maybe crop insurance has come a  long way but i think it probably has a  long way to go yet before it's uh  an effective tool um  you know like for onions we buy crop  insurance because  if you don't have insurance you don't  qualify for any type of disaster aid  should that come down the line  um you know i mentioned you  you have to take the first 50 of loss  you can buy up at substantially higher  premiums um but that just  my opinion doesn't pay either i've  talked to some growers one year  i know i talked to one grower  the payment he received didn't cover his  premiums so  how was that how was that an effective  tool  i'm not sure i think even though i  wouldn't like to spend the extra money  we might have to just to ease the  variability  just like if you were to have a fire you  know you buy fire insurance every year  but hopefully never have a fire  um  i i don't know i sure wouldn't want to  pay 60 more because it's already pretty  expensive but  um  i think i think we might have to um  either that or ask farm credit to  capitalize losses you know in the years  when they  i guess you can do it either way maybe  depending on your banker  well i know a number of years ago i was  reading an article somewhere and  the they're trying to make the point  that  one way or another  consumers are going to have to pay more  for their food whether they pay more at  the grocery store or they pay higher  continue to pay higher subsidies because  uh you know like i said that  the capital that goes into agriculture  these days is uh you know pretty intense  and that then again goes back to the  disconnect between consumers and farmers  a lot of consumers just don't  understand what it what it takes to uh  to grow a crop or to produce food these  days  um  i i think my parents and i usually see  pretty eye to eye on it  actually we just fixed a fixed our  interest rates on a couple of of our  bigger loans  and we were pretty much in agreement  about that  you know we try to  manage some risk and  leave some you know leave some variable  um on the interest rates but then  um on the cropped crop side too you know  you buy crop insurance but it's still a  risky business  um so  i i guess we just look at the the  benefit that we could have from from uh  from trying to mitigate some of the risk  and then the cost of doing that  um  i think  you know uh  perception of risk kind of varies not  only throughout generations but also  throughout individuals um  because i remember taking farm  management and college you know talking  about how uh  you know as the older generation  progresses they get less willing to take  on risk whereas the younger generation  is is more willing um  in case my father  uh i'm not so sure that's true he's  still he's still willing to roll the  dice every year and uh i know he's uh  he's always been forward thinking and  looking ahead to uh next year and i know  over the years he's probably made it  several sacrifices to uh  you know keep us going probably for my  benefit  um so and  you know like jenny mentioned it's  important to have a good working  relationship  you know we talk constantly discuss  these things so um  it's i i think it varies a lot  between businesses and between  individuals  we try to work out all the numbers and  then take a look at it and  make a decision i'm glad that my parents  still make some of those decisions  because they're kind of tough ones  yeah it's sort of an interesting debate  i don't know but i think we ought to  have that debate oh absolutely um i  you know there's i don't think anyone's  gonna argue that we're we're not seeing  more uh  uh  extremes in weather because we certainly  are  um whether you know you  you believe that's due to uh  global warming climate change or if it  just seems to be a  a cycle in a  much larger macro sense  i think there's still a lot of debate  there  um but  yeah i mean we need to continue to have  that debate because uh you know whether  or not  climate change is real uh or how much of  it is uh man-made i think it's uh it's  certainly having an impact on  agriculture  and  i think you know we're gonna have to  respond to it  in some way i don't know  if we're gonna have electric tractors  but i think just uh  agriculture in general is getting more  efficient you feel costs are going up so  of course we're gonna burn  um  look for ways to burn less anyway  yeah i think a lot most farmers are  driven to be efficient by nature um just  you know to save on costs i also worry  that if we have a lot of regulations  imposed on us about climate change  that that we will be less able to  compete with the rest of the world and  like like you were saying before  you know  mexico and china they'll backfill the  food industry  they'll keep the grocery store shelves  full  and  probably what they're doing won't be as  environmentally sound as what we were  doing before so we just we have to be  very careful what we impose on our  farmers i think  but that said i i mean there's maybe  things we should be doing but  maybe it should be more on an  encouragement level than regulatory  we actually had a farm three hours to  the north of us uh when i was a little  girl  my parents did because macintosh grow  better up north  but we struggled because transporting  them back down without bruising was was  really difficult so we we sold the farm  after a few years i think  um  but yeah they we we thought about that  and now we're just tearing macintosh out  but we found a new a new strain that  grows better the ruby mac but  when we've talked we haven't ever talked  really too much about doing that again  because we tried it the one time when  we've talked about relocating it's been  more a discussion about labor  than about climate change  yeah that  that's  i i think probably does make some sense  diversify geographically to  farm in various locations however uh  um from a management side it gets to be  be very difficult um you know if you're  i you know i have no one grower he's uh  this year i guess he's growing some  squash down in the dominican republic so  he's flying back and forth and i just  can't imagine the  the headaches and the costs associated  with some of that i mean certainly  there's advantages but  it's it's a tough thing to do  it's enough trying to handle one  operation  in one country  try and spread that out  okay thank you all and let's give a  round of applause to our panelists  this has been a production of cornell  university on the web at cornell.edu


